From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=EC=8B=AC=EC=83=81=EC=9B=90?= <dev.chamomile@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 13:13:32 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] tests: vmm's unit tests build on Windows

Why: `cargo test -p vmm --lib` did not compile on Windows. Windows'
std::fs::Metadata has no st_dev, st_rdev, st_ino or blocks (stable Rust
cannot read the file id from it), and the VMA lookup the memory tests use
existed for Linux and macOS only.

What: the tests take the identifiers from the handle on Windows, as
build_device_id does (platform::fs::StatIds), and the allocated size from
the handle too (platform::fs::allocated, fstat's st_blocks). A Windows
device id is longer than the 20 bytes the driver receives, so the test
expects its first 20 bytes, which is what build_disk_image_id sends.
find_vma_containing on Windows walks the allocation that contains the
address with VirtualQuery; a free address is in none. Linux and macOS
run the same code as before.

Test: `cargo test -p vmm --lib --no-run` on Windows (MSVC) and macOS;
on Windows, iov_deque, test_set_boot_source, test_get_device_id,
test_update_disk_image, test_guest_region_* and ext:: pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
